        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Two to three years for a semi-transparent stain, three to five for solid, one to two for a clear sealer — but treat those as starting points rather than a schedule. Sun exposure, traffic and drainage move the real answer by a year in either direction, and railings routinely last twice as long as the deck floor.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The water test settles it. Sprinkle water in several places each spring: beading means the finish is still working and the deck needs nothing, while soaking in means it is due. Testing several spots matters, because a walkway often fails a year before the sheltered edges.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Recoat on condition rather than diligently on the calendar, because too often is a real failure mode that builds film and causes peeling. And catch it early: acting when the water test first fails keeps every recoat to a wash and a coat, instead of a sand, a strip and a restoration.
          </p>
        </div>


